androidware: TCG performance for a 32-bit Arm Linux/Android guest (multi-threaded TCG)

Found by profiling an Android 7.0 ARMv7 guest (see docs/optimization.md):

1. SEV/WFE (target/arm/tcg/op_helper.c, translate.c, translate-a64.c)
   Linux/ARM executes SEV on every spin_unlock. helper_sev kicked every other
   vCPU each time (forcing it out of translated code, waking its host thread
   if it idled in WFI). With MTTCG, WFE is now a NOP (the guest spins, as QEMU
   did before WFE/SEV were modelled) and SEV emits no code; without MTTCG SEV
   only wakes CPUs that actually sleep in WFE.

2. Saved address-space contexts (accel/tcg/cputlb.c, include/exec/cputlb.h,
   target/arm/helper.c, target/arm/tcg/tlb-insns.c)
   A change of the ASID (CONTEXTIDR, short-descriptor format) flushed the whole
   softmmu TLB and the jump cache, on every switch between two processes. Each
   vCPU now keeps the TLB tables and jump cache of the last 16 address spaces
   and swaps them. Page invalidations are applied to all of them, any other
   flush drops them, TLBIASID drops the one address space. A context is tagged
   with the TTBR0 it was used with and flushed if the guest reuses the ASID
   with another table. AW_TLB_CTX=0 restores the old behaviour,
   AW_TLB_CTX=verify compares the live TLB with page table walks after every
   switch.

3. Large page tracking (accel/tcg/cputlb.c, include/hw/core/cpu.h)
   The region of large pages is kept as an interval instead of an aligned
   power-of-two block, so that a kmap/vmalloc page flush no longer empties the
   kernel-mode TLB of a guest whose lowmem is mapped with 1 MB sections.

